In this lesson we'll look at why two samples from the same population can give you different numbers, and how the way you display those numbers can change the story. First, sample variation. Even if you use the exact same method, two random samples of fifty from the same population will almost never give you the exact same mean. They might differ by a few percent, and that's completely normal. Each sample is just one draw from a whole range of possible outcomes, and as a rule, bigger samples vary less than smaller ones. Next, representation and persuasion. The display you choose really matters. A truncated axis, one that doesn't start at zero, can make a tiny change look dramatic. A pie chart with too many slices buries the pattern. And cherry-picking, choosing a sample or a time range that supports the story you want to tell, can twist the truth. The same data can look alarming or boring depending on the scale and the chart. Now let's see it in action.